body_text:
**Describe the bug**

I have a unit test for a method that can return multiple subtypes. One of these operation types is expected to be null (since HTTP DELETE operations generally do not return a JSON body). However, null values are not supported by `isNotInstanceOf()`, so I have to use the following.

```java
    // Only patch operation types should return a PatchRequest.
    assertThat(BulkOperation.post("/Users", node).getDataAsScimResource())
        .isNotInstanceOf(PatchRequest.class);

    assertThat(BulkOperation.delete("/Users/userID").getDataAsScimResource())
        .satisfies(data -> assertThat(data instanceof PatchRequest).isFalse());
```

Specifically, if the `satisfies()` call is replaced with `isNotInstanceOf(PatchRequest.class)`, an assertion error is thrown, which is unideal:
```
java.lang.AssertionError: 
Expecting actual not to be null
```
&nbsp;

I would like to suggest changing this behavior. It seems reasonable that if Java considers `null instanceof Object` to always be false, then the following assertions could be considered passing:

```java
    assertThat(null).isNotInstanceOf(String.class);
    assertThat(null).isNotInstanceOfAny(String.class);
    assertThat(null).isNotExactlyInstanceOf(String.class);
```

I'll open a PR with proposed changes to update this behavior if you all agree on whether this seems appropriate. I've confirmed that this code change resolves the issue in my test.
&nbsp;

* assertj core version: 3.27.7
* java version: 17, 21, 25
* test framework version: TestNG 7.12.0
* os (if relevant): N/A

**Test case reproducing the bug**

```java
  @Test
  public void test() {
    String nullString = null;
    assertThat(nullString)
        .satisfies(s -> assertThat(s instanceof String).isFalse())
        .isNotInstanceOf(String.class);
  }
```

