### Summary

I believe I have found a false positive, the bug appears only when doing an import, so in `main.py`.
I wanted to do an abstract class with a property that needs to be set when implemented and which would be used as a condition afterward for running some static methods of the corresponding class. It allows me to easily add another class to do another processing if needed and check if it applies in my case.
This bug appeared in ty 0.0.79 from what I can see.
If you remove the type annotation on the class tuple, it goes away.
It doesn't seem to be flagged by pyrefly, I didn't test with other type checkers.

Here's a playground reproduction: https://play.ty.dev/000e1a6e-2ef1-4e58-ab94-35738b6c01a2

### Version

_No response_

```py
# main.py
from module import class_tuple


for c in class_tuple:
    if c.is_cosmetic:
        print("I am cosmetic")


# module.py
from abc import ABC, abstractmethod


class Base(ABC):
    @property
    @abstractmethod
    def is_cosmetic(self) -> bool:
        pass


class Impl1(Base):
    is_cosmetic = True


class Impl2(Base):
    is_cosmetic = False


class Impl3(Base):
    is_cosmetic = False


class_tuple: tuple[type[Base], ...] = (Impl1, Impl2, Impl3)

for c in class_tuple:
    if c.is_cosmetic:
        print("I am cosmetic")

```

