```py
from typing import Callable, Concatenate, overload, override


class Foo[T, **P, R]:
    def __init__(self, fn: Callable[Concatenate[T, P], R]):
        self.__fn = fn

    @overload
    def __get__(self, instance: T, owner: type[T]) -> Callable[P, R]: ...
    @overload
    def __get__(self, instance: None, owner: type[T]) -> str: ...
    def __get__(self, instance: T | None, owner: type[T]) -> Callable[P, R] | str:
        if instance is None:
            return "asdf"
        def wrapped(*args: P.args, **kwargs: P.kwargs) -> R:
            return self.__fn(instance, *args, **kwargs)
        return wrapped

class Bar:
    @Foo
    def fn(self) -> int: ...

class Baz(Bar):
    @override
    def fn(self) -> int: ... # no error

def fn(bar: type[Bar]) -> None:
    print(bar.fn.capitalize()) # crashes at runtime

fn(Baz)
```

[Playground](https://play.ty.dev/c184a89c-6c25-4f0c-aa65-5d00037f310d)

this crashes at runtime because `Baz.fn` is not a valid subtype of `Bar.fn`.

[pyright catches this](https://basedpyright.com/?typeCheckingMode=all&reportUnusedParameter=false&code=GYJw9gtgBALgngBwJYDsDmUkQWEMoDCAhgDYlEBGJApgDSFgoDGRM1KrdUYAbtSCTBEAJvV78QSYdQCwAKHnym5AM4qoAMTBgA2gBV6AKkMAFegCUAugC55Ue1GnAoAfReokMNwAoV1EsD0wCjWhKTkVNQ6BIwsbBxs%%2BQiii5gt56SMjEbdWbVSp0hL7PbHdLZfLqE0FHJlML1AAhFU2k5aMCXVpuhEWkGoGAQgpVGNqKAJgBe3gTIEyl1OEiksihV2cGd6WfWObzUAAxFAJLgqumUN4KIm8VEKxkk64ylBhyACsECixkEMSEgS9RvAGo27S%2BkgA), however pyright is overly strict when overriding attributes that use descriptors (see https://github.com/DetachHead/basedpyright/issues/1202, and the same issue in ty: #4504). as far as i can tell, pyright is correct to report an error in this case though

