### Prerequisites

- [x] I have written a descriptive issue title.
- [x] I have searched existing issues and pull requests, including closed reports.

### Versions

- Mongoose: 9.11.2, reproduced on master `0f746df5071f7f290723150bd8633d7be9342cb1`
- Node.js: 22.17.1
- MongoDB: 8.2.3
- TypeScript: not applicable (JavaScript)

### Description

After a query or aggregation cursor has initialized, `cursor.destroy(error)` closes the underlying driver cursor but does not emit the supplied `error`. It emits only `close` when driver cleanup succeeds. Consumers using the stream's `error` event therefore miss the reason for destruction.

The stream's `errored` property still contains the error. This report does not claim every consumer, `finished()`, or `pipeline()` incorrectly reports success.

### Steps to reproduce

Run the following against a local MongoDB instance. The connection string can be provided through `MONGOOSE_TEST_URI`.

```js
'use strict';
const mongoose = require('mongoose');

async function main() {
  await mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGOOSE_TEST_URI ||
    'mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mongoose_cursor_destroy_repro');
  try {
    const Model = mongoose.model('DestroyError', new mongoose.Schema({ name: String }));
    for (const kind of ['query', 'aggregate']) {
      const cursor = kind === 'query' ? Model.find().cursor() :
        Model.aggregate([{ $match: {} }]).cursor();
      await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        cursor.once('cursor', resolve);
        cursor.once('error', reject);
      });
      const error = new Error('consumer stopped reading');
      const events = [];
      cursor.on('error', received => events.push(received.message));
      const closed = new Promise(resolve => cursor.once('close', resolve));
      cursor.destroy(error);
      await closed;
      console.log(kind, { events, closed: cursor.closed, errored: cursor.errored === error });
      // Actual: events: [], closed: true, errored: true
      // Expected: events: ['consumer stopped reading'], closed: true, errored: true
    }
  } finally {
    await mongoose.disconnect();
  }
}
main().catch(console.error);
```

### Expected behavior and scope

Forward the supplied error to the `_destroy()` callback after driver cleanup finishes, in accordance with [Node's readable.destroy(error) contract](https://nodejs.org/api/stream.html#readabledestroyerror). Both cursor implementations currently call `callback()` on successful cleanup, discarding the incoming argument.

The proposed fix retains the existing close-error behavior if cleanup itself rejects and retains the awaited close promise from #16562. Related #14966/#14982 added cursor destruction, while #16563 concerns `getDriverCursor()` after an earlier initialization error. Those changes do not forward this destroy argument. I found no matching open fix or explicit decision to suppress this error in the public discussions checked on October 18.

