### Prerequisites

- [x] I have written a descriptive issue title
- [x] I have searched existing issues to ensure the bug has not already been reported

### Mongoose version

9.11.3

### Node.js version

24.16.0

### MongoDB server version

8.2.6

### Typescript version (if applicable)

_No response_

### Description

If a value in the replacement can't be cast, `Model.replaceOne()` leaves that path out and replaces the document anyway, without an error. In the script below `price: 'abc'` for a `Number` path ends up deleting the stored price. `findOneAndReplace()` and `bulkWrite()` with `replaceOne` throw a `ValidationError` for the same replacement.

`Query.prototype._replaceOne()` creates the replacement with `new this.model(...)`, but only reports the cast errors from that document when `runValidators` is set. `_findOneAndReplace()` throws `update.$__.validationError` when `runValidators` isn't set, and also passes the query's `strict` option to the document (added for #13507). `_replaceOne()` doesn't pass `strict` either, so `{ strict: false }` still strips unknown paths and `{ strict: 'throw' }` doesn't throw.

Same on 8.24.6, and on 7.8.7, 6.13.8 and 5.13.23 that I tried. `{ runValidators: true }` works around it.

### Steps to Reproduce

```js
'use strict';

const mongoose = require('mongoose');

run().catch(err => {
  console.error(err);
  process.exit(1);
});

async function run() {
  await mongoose.connect('mongodb://127.0.0.1:27017/mongoose_test');

  const Product = mongoose.model('Product', new mongoose.Schema({
    name: String,
    price: Number
  }));
  await Product.deleteMany({});

  const product = await Product.create({ name: 'Lamp', price: 30 });

  // e.g. a PUT handler that passes req.body to replaceOne()
  const res = await Product.replaceOne({ _id: product._id }, { name: 'Lamp', price: 'abc' });
  console.log('replaceOne() modifiedCount:', res.modifiedCount);
  console.log('stored:', await Product.findById(product._id).lean());

  await Product.updateOne({ _id: product._id }, { price: 30 });
  await Product.findOneAndReplace({ _id: product._id }, { name: 'Lamp', price: 'abc' }).
    catch(err => console.log('findOneAndReplace():', err.message));

  // The strict option is ignored as well
  await Product.replaceOne({ _id: product._id }, { name: 'Lamp', price: 30, color: 'red' }, { strict: false });
  console.log('with strict: false:', await Product.findById(product._id).lean());

  console.log('mongoose', mongoose.version);
  await mongoose.disconnect();
}
```

Output:

```
replaceOne() modifiedCount: 1
stored: { _id: new ObjectId('6ad9aa4a57a2e2fbb98f8628'), name: 'Lamp', __v: 0 }
findOneAndReplace(): Product validation failed: price: Cast to Number failed for value "abc" (type string) at path "price" for model "Product"
with strict: false: {
  _id: new ObjectId('6ad9aa4a57a2e2fbb98f8628'),
  name: 'Lamp',
  price: 30,
  __v: 0
}
mongoose 9.11.3
```

### Expected Behavior

`replaceOne()` throws the same `ValidationError` as `findOneAndReplace()` and leaves the stored document unchanged. With `{ strict: false }`, `color` is kept.

