> This issue was drafted with the help of LLMs (Claude and Codex); I reviewed it and verified the reproductions.

### How are you using Babel?

`@babel/core` Node API with `@babel/plugin-transform-optional-chaining`. No core-js plugin, presets or project configuration.

### Input code

```ts
const receiver = {
  method<T>() {
    return this === receiver;
  }
};
const result = (receiver.method<string>)?.();
console.log(result);
```

This is valid TypeScript. TypeScript 6.0.3 reports no diagnostics and emits a receiver-preserving optional call. The equivalent `receiver.method?.<string>()` is a positive control.

### Configuration

```js
{
  babelrc: false,
  configFile: false,
  parserOpts: { plugins: ['typescript'] },
  plugins: ['@babel/plugin-transform-optional-chaining']
}
```

### Current and expected behavior

Expected: after type erasure, the program prints `true`, as the source does. Type arguments must not change the receiver.

Actual: Babel prints the following call, which produces `false` after type erasure:

```ts
const result = (_ref = receiver.method<string>) === null || _ref === void 0 ? void 0 : _ref();
```

The method is extracted and called without `receiver`. The positive control is lowered with `_receiver$method.call<string>(receiver)` and prints `true`.

This is not limited to TypeScript-to-TypeScript output. An ordinary TypeScript build hits it too: with `presets: ['@babel/preset-typescript'], plugins: ['@babel/plugin-transform-optional-chaining']`, or with `@babel/preset-env` targeting an engine without optional chaining (for example `chrome 79` or `ie 11`) together with `@babel/preset-typescript`, the call becomes plain JavaScript, `(_ref = receiver.method) === null || _ref === void 0 ? void 0 : _ref()`, and Node prints `false` for the output, while Node's own type stripping of the source prints `true`.

### Standalone reproduction

Save the input as `input.ts`, and save this as `reproduce.mjs`:

```js
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { transformSync } from '@babel/core';
import ts from 'typescript';
import { runInNewContext } from 'node:vm';

const input = readFileSync('input.ts', 'utf8');
const { code } = transformSync(input, {
  babelrc: false,
  configFile: false,
  parserOpts: { plugins: ['typescript'] },
  plugins: ['@babel/plugin-transform-optional-chaining']
});
for (const source of [input, code]) {
  const js = ts.transpileModule(source, {
    compilerOptions: { target: ts.ScriptTarget.ES2022 }
  }).outputText;
  runInNewContext(js, { console });
}
```

In an empty directory, with Node 26.12.0:

```sh
npm install --save-exact @babel/core@8.0.7 @babel/plugin-transform-optional-chaining@8.0.5 typescript@6.0.3
node reproduce.mjs
```

Expected: `true`, `true`. Actual: `true`, `false`.

Also reproduced after installing `@babel/core@7.29.7` and `@babel/plugin-transform-optional-chaining@7.29.7` instead.

### Possible cause and Babel 7 interaction

`isTransparentExprWrapper` in `@babel/helper-skip-transparent-expression-wrappers` does not include `TSInstantiationExpression`. Optional-call lowering therefore does not recognize the member callee behind the instantiation. See the [helper source](https://github.com/babel/babel/blob/v8.0.7/packages/babel-helper-skip-transparent-expression-wrappers/src/index.ts#L26-L37). Adding `TSInstantiationExpression` to that list makes both the configuration above and the `@babel/preset-typescript` build print `true` on Babel 8.0.7 and 7.29.7 (checked by patching the built helper).

There is a second way to reach this same failure without core-js: parse and print the positive control, then run optional-call lowering in a separate transform. Babel 7 prints `receiver.method?.<string>()` as `receiver.method<string>?.()`. That is valid TypeScript and type erasure alone still preserves the receiver; the subsequent Babel optional-call transform loses it. Babel 8 preserves the modern spelling and the two-transform control succeeds. This is recorded here as another route into the missing-wrapper handling, rather than as a separate semantic printer report.

This reproduction uses an ordinary member optional call, without super-call lowering or assertion expressions.

### Environment and verification

Verified 2026-10-19 on Node 26.12.0, macOS arm64, TypeScript 6.0.3:

| Core | Parser | Generator | Optional chaining | Source | Lowered | Direct modern control | Reprint then lower |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 8.0.7 | 8.0.7 | 8.0.6 | 8.0.5 | true | false | true | true |
| 7.29.7 | 7.29.9 | 7.29.8 | 7.29.7 | true | false | true | false |

