export = value;
```

This input passes TypeScript 6.0.3 typechecking. The imported name is deliberately unused, so the reproduction does not depend on reading a type-only binding as a runtime value.

### Configuration

```js
{
  babelrc: false,
  configFile: false,
  parserOpts: { plugins: ['typescript'] }
}
```

### Current and expected behavior

Expected: the output retains `import type X = require('./only-types')`. A later TypeScript compilation removes the declaration and the program prints `loaded` without loading a module.

Actual: Babel prints:

```ts
import X = require('./only-types');
console.log('loaded');
```

The input AST correctly has `TSImportEqualsDeclaration.importKind === 'type'`. Parsing the printed text yields `importKind === 'value'`. This loss occurs during printing, without any transform plugin. `export import type X = require('./only-types')` loses the modifier in the same way.

With TypeScript `verbatimModuleSyntax: true` and CommonJS output, the printed declaration becomes a runtime `require('./only-types')`. The source loads successfully; the reprinted program throws `MODULE_NOT_FOUND` before logging. If a JavaScript module exists, it is loaded and its side effects run unexpectedly instead. Without `verbatimModuleSyntax`, TypeScript may remove the unused value import and mask the printing defect.

### Standalone reproduction

Save the two input files above and this `reproduce.mjs`:

