> This issue was drafted with the help of LLMs (Claude and Codex); I reviewed it and verified the reproductions.

### How are you using Babel?

`@babel/core` Node API, parsing and printing TypeScript without stripping types. No plugins, presets, core-js or project configuration.

### Input code

Example A, a runtime-visible grouping change (`input.ts`):

```ts
const f = <T>(value: T): T => value;
const g = <T>(value: T): T => value;
const result = ((f || g)<string>)('ok');
console.log(typeof result, result === 'ok');
```

Example B, a terminal instantiation with a type-visible grouping change (`input-types.ts`):

```ts
declare const f: <T>() => T;
declare const g: <T>() => T;
const v = (f || g)<string>;
```

Both inputs pass TypeScript 6.0.3 typechecking without diagnostics.

### Configuration

```js
{
  babelrc: false,
  configFile: false,
  parserOpts: { plugins: ['typescript'] }
}
```

### Current and expected behavior

Expected: the logical expression remains the operand of the instantiation, and Example A calls the selected function. It prints `string true`; the type of `v` in Example B is `() => string`.

Actual: Babel removes the parentheses and prints these expressions:

```ts
const result = f || g<string>('ok');
const v = f || g<string>;
```

The instantiation and call now apply only to `g`. Since `f` is truthy, Example A returns the function `f` without calling it. After type erasure it prints `function false`. A full TypeScript check of the printed Example A also reports TS2367 for comparing that function with a string; this is an error introduced by the reprint, not an error in the source.

Example B remains valid TypeScript but the type of `v` changes to `<T>() => T`, losing the explicit string specialization. Thus type erasure can hide the terminal form even though its type meaning changed.

### Standalone reproduction

Save Example A as `input.ts`, and save this as `reproduce.mjs`:

```js
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { transformSync } from '@babel/core';
import ts from 'typescript';
import { runInNewContext } from 'node:vm';

const input = readFileSync('input.ts', 'utf8');
const { code } = transformSync(input, {
  babelrc: false,
  configFile: false,
  parserOpts: { plugins: ['typescript'] }
});
console.log(code);
for (const source of [input, code]) {
  const js = ts.transpileModule(source, {
    compilerOptions: { target: ts.ScriptTarget.ES2022 }
  }).outputText;
  runInNewContext(js, { console });
}
```

In an empty directory, with Node 26.12.0:

```sh
npm install --save-exact @babel/core@8.0.7 typescript@6.0.3
node reproduce.mjs
```

After the printed TypeScript, the script logs `string true` for the source and `function false` for the output. Also reproduced with `@babel/core@7.29.7`.

### Possible cause and scope

The parentheses decision for a logical expression does not retain its grouping as the operand of `TSInstantiationExpression`; the [instantiation printer](https://github.com/babel/babel/blob/v8.0.7/packages/babel-generator/src/generators/typescript.ts#L579-L586) then prints the operand followed by the type arguments. Only the operand grouping matters: dropping just the outer parentheses would be harmless, because `(f<string>)('ok')` and `f<string>('ok')` mean the same.

The cause is [`hasPostfixPart`](https://github.com/babel/babel/blob/v8.0.7/packages/babel-generator/src/node/parentheses.ts#L59-L75) in the generator's parentheses rules: it does not treat `TSInstantiationExpression` as a postfix context, so every lower-precedence operand loses its parentheses. `(a ? f : g)<string>` is printed as `a ? f : g<string>`, and `(await f)<string>`, `(void f)<string>`, `(f = g)<string>` and `(f + g)<string>` change in the same way. Adding that case keeps the parentheses in all of these inputs (checked by patching the built generator on Babel 8.0.7 and 7.29.7).

The printed code reparses successfully with the wrong grouping.

### Environment and verification

Verified 2026-10-19 on Node 26.12.0, macOS arm64, TypeScript 6.0.3:

| Core | Parser | Generator | Source runtime | Printed runtime | Source v type | Printed v type |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 8.0.7 | 8.0.7 | 8.0.6 | `string true` | `function false` | `() => string` | `<T>() => T` |
| 7.29.7 | 7.29.9 | 7.29.8 | `string true` | `function false` | `() => string` | `<T>() => T` |

A simple instantiation such as `f<string>` is printed correctly.

